        "For iron rusting, anodic iron dissolution and cathodic oxygen reduction occur at different surface regions; water and ions complete the circuit.\n\nAlkaline KMnO4 or acidified K2Cr2O7 oxidises ethanol first to ethanal and then ethanoic acid. Combustion is more extensive oxidation to CO2 and water when oxygen is sufficient.",
        "zinc coating protects steel both as a barrier and, if scratched, by oxidizing sacrificially before iron.\n\nExample: Warming ethanol with an oxidising agent changes it toward ethanoic acid; the product has acidic properties unlike the starting alcohol.",
        "anode: Fe -> Fe2+ + 2e-\n\nCH3CH2OH + 2[O] -> CH3COOH + H2O",
        "Rusting requires an electrochemical pathway; oxygen alone without suitable moisture is insufficient for ordinary rust formation.\n\nDo not assume adding visible oxygen gas is the only oxidation pathway; oxidising agents transfer oxygen or remove hydrogen chemically.",
        "Corrosion science protects bridges, pipelines, vehicles, ships and electronic equipment.\n\nControlled oxidation produces acids, aldehydes and industrial intermediates, while uncontrolled oxidation affects fuels and food spoilage.",
