    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Carbon Compounds|Covalent bonding in carbon",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A covalent bond forms when atoms share one or more pairs of electrons.\n\nElectron-dot structures show valence electrons and the pairs transferred or shared during compound formation.",
        "Shared electron density attracts both nuclei. Single, double and triple bonds differ in shared pairs, length and strength.\n\nWrite the correct number of valence dots around each element symbol. For covalent molecules, place shared pairs between bonded atoms and lone pairs on the appropriate atoms. For ionic compounds, show bracketed ions with charges after electron transfer.",
        "in H2, each hydrogen contributes one electron to a shared pair, allowing both atoms to achieve a stable duet.\n\nExample: In H2O, oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen and retains two lone pairs. For NaCl, show [Na]+ and [Cl]- with chlorine's complete octet.",
        "bond order: single < double < triple\n\none shared pair = one covalent bond; bracket plus charge = ion",
        "Sharing is not always perfectly equal; electronegativity differences can make a covalent bond polar.\n\nDo not draw core electrons or omit ionic charges; electron-dot structures focus on valence electrons and must conserve the total electron count.",
        "Covalent bonding creates molecules, polymers, biomolecules and network solids.\n\nDot structures provide a bridge from valency and formulas to bonding, shape and chemical reactivity.",
        listOf("in H2, each hydrogen contributes one electron to a shared pair, allowing both atoms to achieve a stable duet.","Example: In H2O, oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen and retains two lone pairs.","For NaCl, show [Na]+ and [Cl]- with chlorine's complete octet.","bond order: single < double < triple\n\none shared pair = one covalent bond; bracket plus charge = ion","Shared electron density attracts both nuclei.","Single, double and triple bonds differ in shared pairs, length and strength."),
        listOf("Electronegativity","Lewis structures","Bond polarity","Valence electrons","Lewis symbols","Octet rule","Covalent bond","Electron-dot structures")),
