    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Carbon Compounds|Ethanol and ethanoic acid",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Alcohols are prepared by hydration or reduction and react through the O-H bond and C-O bond in oxidation, dehydration, esterification and substitution.\n\nCarboxylic acids form by oxidation or hydrolysis and react through acidity and nucleophilic acyl substitution to make salts, esters, acyl halides and amides.",
        "Alkenes hydrate to alcohols; aldehydes and ketones reduce to primary and secondary alcohols. Primary alcohol oxidation gives aldehydes then acids, secondary gives ketones, while tertiary alcohols resist mild oxidation. Concentrated acid causes dehydration, and carboxylic acids give esters reversibly.\n\nOxidation of primary alcohols or aldehydes and hydrolysis of nitriles produce acids. Resonance stabilises carboxylate ions. Acids react with bicarbonate releasing CO2, esterify alcohols reversibly, and decarboxylate as sodium salts with soda lime. Electron-withdrawing substituents increase acidity.",
        "CH3CH2OH + [O] -> CH3CHO + H2O -> CH3COOH. With ethanoic acid and acid catalyst it forms ethyl ethanoate and water.\n\nExample: CH3COOH + NaHCO3 -> CH3COONa + CO2 + H2O. CH3COOH + C2H5OH <=> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O with H+ and heat.",
        "RCH2OH + [O] -> RCHO -> RCOOH; ROH + R'COOH <=> R'COOR + H2O\n\nRCOOH + ROH <=> RCOOR + H2O; RCOONa + NaOH -> RH + Na2CO3",
        "Do not state that all alcohols oxidise to carboxylic acids; the product depends on whether the alcohol is primary, secondary or tertiary.\n\nDo not confuse alcohol acidity with carboxylic-acid acidity or forget that Fischer esterification is an equilibrium requiring acid catalyst.",
        "Alcohol reactions support solvents, fuels, antiseptics, fragrances, polymers and pharmaceutical synthesis.\n\nCarboxylic-acid derivatives occur in medicines, polymers, food preservatives, fats, soaps and fragrances.",
        listOf("CH3CH2OH + [O] -> CH3CHO + H2O -> CH3COOH.","With ethanoic acid and acid catalyst it forms ethyl ethanoate and water.","Example: CH3COOH + NaHCO3 -> CH3COONa + CO2 + H2O.","CH3COOH + C2H5OH <=> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O with H+ and heat.","RCH2OH + [O] -> RCHO -> RCOOH; ROH + R'COOH <=> R'COOR + H2O\n\nRCOOH + ROH <=> RCOOR + H2O; RCOONa + NaOH -> RH + Na2CO3","Alkenes hydrate to alcohols"),
        listOf("Oxidation","Dehydration","Esterification","Acidity","Decarboxylation","Preparation and reactions of alcohols","Preparation and reactions of carboxylic acids")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Carbon Compounds|Soaps and detergents",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Soap ions have a water-attracting ionic head and an oil-attracting hydrocarbon tail, allowing them to surround grease in micelles.\n\nSynthetic detergents clean effectively in hard water because their calcium and magnesium salts remain much more soluble than soap scum.",
        "Above a suitable concentration in water, tails cluster inward around oily dirt while charged heads face outward. Agitation breaks grease into suspended droplets that can be rinsed away.\n\nSoap carboxylate ions react with Ca2+ or Mg2+ to form insoluble scum, wasting soap and reducing lather. Sulfonate or sulfate detergent heads generally avoid this precipitation, though detergent biodegradability and phosphate pollution must be considered.",
        "When oily cloth is washed with soap, micelles encapsulate oil and keep droplets dispersed instead of redepositing them on the fabric.\n\nExample: Soap lathers poorly in hard water until Ca2+/Mg2+ are removed, while a suitable detergent continues forming micelles and cleaning.",
        "hydrophobic tails -> grease core; hydrophilic COO- heads -> water\n\n2RCOO- + Ca2+ -> (RCOO)2Ca(s) scum",
        "Do not say soap chemically destroys all grease; its principal cleansing action emulsifies and suspends oily material.\n\nDo not claim every detergent is environmentally harmless merely because it works in hard water; composition and biodegradability matter.",
        "Micelles enable washing, emulsification and formulation of cleaners and personal-care products.\n\nDetergents support domestic and industrial cleaning, while formulation choices affect wastewater and aquatic ecosystems.",
        listOf("When oily cloth is washed with soap, micelles encapsulate oil and keep droplets dispersed instead of redepositing them on the fabric.","Example: Soap lathers poorly in hard water until Ca2+/Mg2+ are removed, while a suitable detergent continues forming micelles and cleaning.","hydrophobic tails -> grease core; hydrophilic COO- heads -> water\n\n2RCOO- + Ca2+ -> (RCOO)2Ca(s) scum","Above a suitable concentration in water, tails cluster inward around oily dirt while charged heads face outward.","Agitation breaks grease into suspended droplets that can be rinsed away.","Soap carboxylate ions react with Ca2+ or Mg2+ to form insoluble scum, wasting soap and reducing lather."),
        listOf("Amphiphiles","Emulsification","Surface chemistry","Hardness ions","Soap scum","Biodegradability","Soap micelles and cleansing action","Detergents and hard water")),
